===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|brytten|lang=enm}}, {{term|brutten|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|brittian|lang=ang}}, {{term|bryttian||to divide, dispense, distribute, rule over, possess, enjoy the use of|lang=ang}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*brutjaną||to break, divide}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰreud-||to break}}. Cognate with {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|brytja||to chop up, break in pieces, slaughter|lang=is}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|bryta||to break, fracture, cut off|lang=sv}}, {{etyl|da|-}} {{term|bryde||to break|lang=da}} and Albanian {{term|brydh||I make crumbly, friable, soft|lang=sq}}. Related to {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|brytta||dispenser, giver, author, governor, prince|lang=ang}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{m|ang|brēotan||to break in pieces, hew down, demolish, destroy, kill}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l/en|britt}}
* {{l/en|brite}} {{qualifier|dialectal}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|britt|ing}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[break]] in pieces; [[divide]].
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[bruise]]; [[indent]].
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To [[fall]] out or [[shatter]] (as overripe hops or grain).
# {{context|intransitive|dialectal|lang=en}} To [[fade]] away; [[alter]].

